<Base title="Method" description="How a Squirrel-Proof Feeder Index page gets written.">
  <h1>Method</h1>
  <p>One operator, one niche. No team, no invented company story.</p>
  <h2>What a page needs</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>A real product. Manufacturer page is the first source.</li>
    <li>Independent tests when they exist. We name the test length.</li>
    <li>What it is, who it is for, standouts, drawbacks, who should skip, verdict, sources.</li>
    <li>If a spec is not on a source, we write unknown.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What we will not do</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>Star ratings or composite scores.</li>
    <li>Invented brands, prices, reviews, or traffic numbers.</li>
    <li>Rubber-stamp “squirrel-proof.” Cornell FeederWatch says squirrels often get in. We say resistant.</li>
    <li>Affiliate links until this site has real traffic and the owner opens Phase 4.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One listing at a time. No thin batches.</p>
</Base>
